                    "source": "Twitter Web App",
                    "text": "Washington points at insurers now, but it rarely mentions the congressional decisions that helped those profits climb in the first place.\n\nHere\u2019s what never gets mentioned:\n\n\u2022 In 2010, Congress wrote the ACA to run through private insurers no public option, no competitive alternative because industry lobbyists helped shape the final structure. \n\nThat decision guaranteed rising stock valuations before a single policy was sold.\n\n\u2022 From 2017\u20132020, congressional repeal attempts blew holes in the system: cutting CSR payments and ending the mandate penalty forced insurers to raise premiums nationwide, a spike Congress had been warned about in GAO and CBO briefings.\n\n\u2022 And by 2023\u20132024, the top five insurers had posted over $300 billion in cumulative profits, while Congress never passed the transparency and competition reforms regulators requested to keep premiums from rising another 100\u2013150% for many families.\n\nSo yes, insurers made a fortune.\n\nBut no talking point changes the fact that Congress both parties built a system where insurers win first, families pay second, and accountability shows up last.\n\nAnd now you know\u2026 the rest of the story.\n\n@HouseGOP @SenateGOP @HouseDemocrats @SenateDems",
                    "text_lang": "en",
